[Music]  russia's invasion of ukraine has  prompted an unprecedented response from  the west  economic warfare the european union and  its partners are working to [ __ ]  putin's ability to finance his war  machine we are resolved to continue  imposing massive costs on russia western  sanctions have sent shock waves across  russia's economy the um ruble has sunk  to another record low but will it be  enough to stop putin our experts weigh  in  what sanctions have been imposed on  russia and how severe are they  one category is financial sanctions  there they've been various measures  targeting russian banks  uh a number of them have been blocked  from swift which is a messaging system  for cross-border payments and then on  top of the the banking sanctions there's  uh a number of measures involving  sovereign debt including restrictions to  secondary trading of of russian  sovereign debt and um  uh russian companies and banks have been  have had their access to to western  financial markets cut  but the biggest bazooka of all on the  financial front  is um the sanctions against the the  central bank of russia the third  category i'd say is measures targeting  um vladimir putin himself members of his  circle  and a number of oligarchs who are seen  as either being close to him or  supportive of the kremlin and you know  there we've seen asset freezes travel  bans and so on and the thing uh matthew  um i'm sure will agree with this is you  know the thing that really  made a big difference is this uh threat  to freeze the  630 billion dollars of  assets that the central bank holds  overseas  and that's really  the sort of safety net that  backstops entire economy what effect  have sanctions had so far and what's  likely to happen to the russian economy  the effect of sanctions is really to  create a kind of heart attack  in russia's financial system and that's  the first and most important effect so  you've seen the ruble  fall  as people worry about capital flight  worry that the central bank no longer  has the foreign assets it needs to  defend the currency the share prices  of russian  firms that are traded in moscow or  offshore have  collapsed including the banks and all of  that filters through into a kind of  generalized loss of confidence in the  financial system and there are cues of  russians  outside  bank branches across russia at the  moment beyond that i think a big  question mark is how much the energy  sector and trade in oil and gas is  affected by this  they have notionally been excluded from  the sanctions regime but the complexity  of dealing with russia in order to buy  russian oil gas seems to be putting a  lot of people off which is why the oil  price has now hit 110 dollars roughly  yeah i think that that's right patrick  and i'd add a couple of things to that  one is you know that um well according  to some estimates and what one one of  them is based on real-time data we're  seeing inflation spiking to to quite an  alarming level um by one estimate um 70  on an annual basis which is obviously  something to watch very closely and and  something that will um impact very  heavily on on ordinary russians um and a  broader point to make is that you know  this really  shows that the the strategy that russia  had the fortress russia strategy they  had as they called it which was to  insulate the economy from  sanctions which they they introduced  almost a decade ago after earlier  sanctions in relation to crimea that  that really looks like a potemkin  structure  it's crumbled away  what has russia's response been so far  what russia's sought to do  is try and invent a playbook  off the cuff to deal with having a major  financial crisis  uh on its hands and  the variety of stabilization  things that they've tried to do in the  financial markets to restore confidence  but there's not a magic bullet for them  because the bulk of their foreign assets  which is the backstop of the system are  under threat of being frozen the big  question is whether russia chooses to  escalate  not just in a military fashion which is  what it's doing in ukraine right now but  in an economic fashion against the west  it could try and curtail gas  exports to europe that's a real pressure  point or a various specialist metals  where russia's exports are pretty  important at global supply chains or  cyber attacks could be another uh tool  attacking western uh economic rather  than military interests in terms of the  the the um economic retaliation it's  been very muted so far i mean one of the  only things that russia has done is to  um  to  announce a tit-for-tat ban on um planes  from a number of countries using its  airspace and um  and and using its airports and um  interestingly um if  russia does manage to  take full control of ukraine  it could hold back things like rare  gases which ukraine is a big supplier of  and which are used in the manufacture of  semiconductors they're they're important  in um semiconductor etching so that's  just one example of a lever it could  pull um  [Music]  on on the basis of taking control of  ukraine if it manages to do that will  economic warfare help to stop putin  i don't think anyone including western  leaders and officials um thought that  sanctions alone would persuade vladimir  putin to pull his troops out of ukraine  i don't think anyone was naive enough to  think that um  you know even  dramatic financial sanctions would would  on their own do that  but sanctions are about several things  really uh and you know one is i suppose  on a basic level just showing that  there's a cost to be paid  um that if you're um you know if you  engage in in something as reckless as as  as you know invading a neighboring  country that  you're going to pay a price the glass  half empty view on this sanctions have  already kind of failed they they failed  to deter the invasion of  of ukraine and will probably fail  uh to uh lead russia to to you know end  that invasion and and  retreat back uh within its own borders  but i think the glass um half full uh  view is rather different and it's that  uh in the short term uh sanctions  change the incentives potentially of  russia's leadership and mr putin  and that  it creates a dynamic where if they scale  back the violence in ukraine  there can be a reciprocal scaling back  partial scaling back of economic  sanctions so it gives the west an  ongoing lever with which to  try to influence russia's behavior and  it may be that that lever yields results  if it does not and russia continues on  its path of action in ukraine causing  devastation then sanctions still have a  different function which is to  significantly weaken russia's economic  capabilities and in the end that will  have a significant impact on its  military capabilities over time  thank you for watching  for more of our coverage on ukraine  please click on the link and don't  forget to subscribe

